WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.220 --> 00:00:09.073
Open Terminal on Ubuntu. We will use this GPU to
evaluate ten 7D12 nanobody variants against EGFR.

2
00:00:09.233 --> 00:00:13.671
Run nvidia-smi and check that
your GPU and driver appear.

3
00:00:14.137 --> 00:00:21.881
Download the public release with Git.
git clone --branch v0.4.8 https://github.com/kangk1204/NanoCoFold_public.git

4
00:00:22.041 --> 00:00:26.627
If Ubuntu says Git is missing, run
sudo apt install git and try again.

5
00:00:27.095 --> 00:00:31.788
Wait for the terminal prompt,
then type cd NanoCoFold_public.

6
00:00:35.762 --> 00:00:40.327
Check the version. Your terminal should print v0.4.8.

7
00:00:43.053 --> 00:00:48.813
Use the screen installation profile. The check-host
option checks the hardware before installing.

8
00:00:51.095 --> 00:00:57.090
For a fresh Ubuntu installation, run
bash install.sh --profile screen.

9
00:00:57.250 --> 00:01:02.498
This profile provides Boltz-2 for prediction
and ANARCI for numbering the nanobody.

10
00:01:02.658 --> 00:01:08.290
The install profile names the tools. In the
app, you will choose Evaluate nanobody variants.

11
00:01:08.450 --> 00:01:14.594
Omit --skip-system-packages on a new computer.
Enter your sudo password if Ubuntu asks.

12
00:01:14.754 --> 00:01:19.490
This computer already has the system
packages, so its command includes that option.

13
00:01:19.650 --> 00:01:24.962
Leave this terminal open while the installer
prepares the tools and downloads their files.

14
00:01:25.122 --> 00:01:30.071
Once installation finishes on your computer,
you can jump to the Start the web app chapter.

15
00:11:08.132 --> 00:11:13.700
Wait for the installation complete message
and the instruction to run bash start.sh.

16
00:11:14.178 --> 00:11:20.173
After installation finishes, run bash start.sh
in this folder and keep the terminal open.

17
00:11:20.333 --> 00:11:24.621
On the same computer, open the web
address printed next to NanoCoFold.

18
00:11:31.345 --> 00:11:36.230
Open the public NanoCoFold GitHub
repository in a new browser tab.

19
00:11:36.390 --> 00:11:38.822
On the repository page, click Releases.

20
00:11:41.891 --> 00:11:47.565
Select release v0.4.8 to find the
files that go with this lesson.

21
00:11:51.720 --> 00:11:58.525
On the v0.4.8 release page, scroll down
to Assets, the list of downloadable files.

22
00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:10.777
Download NanoCoFold-EGFR-10-inputs.zip
and save it in Downloads.

23
00:12:14.137 --> 00:12:17.785
Keep the first terminal open.
It is running NanoCoFold.

24
00:12:17.945 --> 00:12:23.086
In Files, open Downloads. Right-click an
empty area and choose Open in Terminal.

25
00:12:23.246 --> 00:12:28.131
In this second terminal, extract the
ZIP into a folder named EGFR-lesson.

26
00:12:35.647 --> 00:12:41.769
Enter the extracted folder. Keep it open so you can
select the structure and copy the two sequences.

27
00:12:45.407 --> 00:12:53.215
This is the target protein. Copy both lines,
including the greater-than sign and B, when the app
asks for the full target sequence.

28
00:12:57.737 --> 00:13:05.033
This second sequence is the original nanobody. It goes in
the separate wild-type field. Keep the two files distinct.

29
00:13:10.178 --> 00:13:14.146
This example uses the experimental structure 4KRL.

30
00:13:14.306 --> 00:13:21.368
The larger protein is our target, EGFR. The
smaller one is the original 7D12 nanobody.

31
00:13:25.008 --> 00:13:30.022
A protein sequence is a list of letters.
Each letter stands for one amino acid.

32
00:13:30.182 --> 00:13:34.662
The practice ZIP contains the target
and nanobody sequences from this entry.

33
00:13:38.053 --> 00:13:43.045
Open hotspots.csv in a spreadsheet,
or read it in the terminal with cat.

34
00:13:43.205 --> 00:13:47.728
The first column contains the positions
you will enter into NanoCoFold.

35
00:13:50.473 --> 00:13:55.444
Count the target sequence from one. Its
19th letter is L, which stands for leucine.

36
00:13:55.604 --> 00:14:03.156
That same amino acid is residue 325 in the
original structure. Type 19 in this exercise.

37
00:14:04.473 --> 00:14:12.132
Use 19, 40 and 42. These three target residues are
close to the nanobody in the experimental structure.

38
00:14:12.292 --> 00:14:15.535
Leave the hotspot recommendation exclusion box empty.

39
00:14:18.473 --> 00:14:21.055
Later you will change two letters on the nanobody.

40
00:14:21.215 --> 00:14:25.865
The three target positions you just chose
stay the same in all eleven predictions.

41
00:14:31.512 --> 00:14:35.181
On the Input and run tab, choose
the analysis you want to run.

42
00:14:37.220 --> 00:14:40.079
In the web app, choose Evaluate nanobody variants.

43
00:14:40.239 --> 00:14:45.849
We already have a nanobody, 7D12. We want
to compare small changes to its sequence.

44
00:14:46.009 --> 00:14:52.559
Name the run EGFR 10 variants walkthrough. We
will use this name again in the results database.

45
00:14:53.012 --> 00:14:59.390
Select structure input, then choose a file.
In Downloads, open your EGFR-lesson folder.

46
00:14:59.550 --> 00:15:05.417
Select 4KRL.cif. Back in
NanoCoFold, click Load structure.

47
00:15:05.887 --> 00:15:11.327
Choose the epidermal growth factor receptor
as the target. The app displays it as chain B.

48
00:15:11.487 --> 00:15:17.503
In the deposited structure, its author chain
identifier is A. Use B for this input file.

49
00:15:17.663 --> 00:15:21.673
Leave the partner boxes empty and
click Review chains and exposure.

50
00:15:22.137 --> 00:15:27.065
Choose Predict from full sequences. Keep
Boltz-2 as the target structure engine.

51
00:15:27.225 --> 00:15:33.049
Paste both lines from EGFR-target.fasta,
including the header >B.

52
00:15:33.209 --> 00:15:40.761
For this exercise, use all 214 amino acids
of the EGFR fragment in the structure entry.

53
00:15:40.921 --> 00:15:47.811
The first line names the chain. On the next line,
each letter stands for one amino acid in EGFR.

54
00:15:48.262 --> 00:15:51.568
Select Map selected hotspots
to full-sequence positions.

55
00:15:51.728 --> 00:15:59.387
Enter 19, 40, 42. These are three positions on
EGFR where we want the nanobody to make contact.

56
00:15:59.547 --> 00:16:05.968
Enter just the sites of interest. Leave the other
positions out. The EGFR sequence stays the same.

57
00:16:06.128 --> 00:16:11.483
If you have only a target sequence, NanoCoFold
can suggest sites from its predicted structure.

58
00:16:11.928 --> 00:16:17.027
Open the numbering table. The first residue
of the input sequence is position one.

59
00:16:17.187 --> 00:16:26.680
Positions 19, 40 and 42 correspond to
author residue numbers 325, 346 and 348.

60
00:16:26.840 --> 00:16:29.848
Use the input sequence numbers in the hotspot field.

61
00:16:31.095 --> 00:16:34.295
Review the structure notes and
tick the acknowledgment box.

62
00:16:34.455 --> 00:16:41.004
Paste 7D12-WT.fasta into the wild-type
nanobody field. Then open Run settings.

63
00:16:50.470 --> 00:16:55.291
Under the exploration preset, choose
Legacy default. Open the variant settings.

64
00:16:55.451 --> 00:17:01.339
Choose random substitutions. For the
region, choose CDR3, a loop on the nanobody.

65
00:17:01.499 --> 00:17:08.731
This is where we change amino acids on 7D12.
The EGFR hotspots you chose earlier stay fixed.

66
00:17:09.178 --> 00:17:15.002
Request ten different variants. Set both the
maximum and minimum number of substitutions to two.

67
00:17:15.162 --> 00:17:20.325
Each variant will differ from the
original at exactly two CDR3 positions.

68
00:17:22.387 --> 00:17:29.171
Include WT, the original 7D12 with no changes.
It gives us a reference to compare against.

69
00:17:29.331 --> 00:17:31.976
Choose Boltz-2 as the evaluation engine.

70
00:17:32.136 --> 00:17:37.235
Keep seed 42. It sets the starting point
for choosing the random substitutions.

71
00:17:37.395 --> 00:17:41.235
Ten variants plus one wild type
give eleven planned models.

72
00:17:46.470 --> 00:17:52.891
Continue to input review and review the applied
settings. Check the run name, engine and eleven models.

73
00:17:53.051 --> 00:17:59.003
MSA means multiple sequence alignment. This run
looks for saved alignments on this computer.

74
00:17:59.163 --> 00:18:03.174
No sequences are sent to a
public MSA server for this run.

75
00:18:03.637 --> 00:18:08.309
From this input review, choose Go to
environment check, then Check environment.

76
00:18:08.469 --> 00:18:13.354
The app now checks the tools needed for this
specific run. Wait for Environment ready.

77
00:18:33.803 --> 00:18:38.689
When Environment ready appears, choose Prepare
analysis to return to the checked inputs.

78
00:18:52.678 --> 00:18:57.841
Click Run analysis once. Leave the page
open while the server accepts the request.

79
00:18:58.001 --> 00:19:03.910
The structure file has coordinates
for 205 of the 214 target residues.

80
00:19:04.070 --> 00:19:10.684
This run uses the full protein sequence. Crystal
sugars and other nonprotein components are omitted.

81
00:19:10.844 --> 00:19:16.625
The form also confirms ten distinct variants
are available, with one wild-type control added.

82
00:19:24.012 --> 00:19:29.814
Here the browser could not confirm the request
in time. The message directs us to Jobs and logs.

83
00:19:29.974 --> 00:19:34.518
Before trying again, refresh the jobs
list and look for your original run name.

84
00:19:37.137 --> 00:19:41.531
If you are back at the home screen,
open Jobs and logs, then click Refresh.

85
00:19:41.691 --> 00:19:45.915
Look for EGFR 10 variants walkthrough,
the name you entered earlier.

86
00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:55.776
Select the entry. Running confirms that the
server received this job. Leave it running.

87
00:19:55.936 --> 00:20:01.675
Two out of three counts workflow stages. The
prediction line below will count individual models.

88
00:20:04.012 --> 00:20:09.836
The first model has finished. The line reads one
of eleven and shows an estimated time remaining.

89
00:20:09.996 --> 00:20:13.665
We will skip ahead through the time
spent waiting for the other predictions.

90
00:20:14.137 --> 00:20:17.678
The same job has now completed
six of its eleven models.

91
00:20:22.470 --> 00:20:28.934
The complete run took 17 minutes and 48
seconds on this RTX 3060. Your time may differ.

92
00:20:29.094 --> 00:20:33.297
When the status changes to Complete,
click Open results on this same job.

93
00:20:33.457 --> 00:20:38.150
The report will show which models were
evaluated and which met the selection criteria.

94
00:20:38.928 --> 00:20:42.747
In Inputs and settings, check the
analysis name and mature mode.

95
00:20:42.907 --> 00:20:45.958
We asked for a cached alignment of related sequences.

96
00:20:46.118 --> 00:20:51.344
The report says No MSA: the predictions
actually used only the input sequences.

97
00:20:51.504 --> 00:20:54.747
Use the same engine and conditions for comparisons.

98
00:20:55.220 --> 00:20:58.185
Open section two, Candidate and model counts.

99
00:20:58.345 --> 00:21:02.100
Ten mutants and the original
nanobody make eleven sequences.

100
00:21:02.260 --> 00:21:06.484
11 models were evaluated. The
recorded selection count is 0.

101
00:21:10.762 --> 00:21:13.300
Open section three to compare candidates.

102
00:21:13.460 --> 00:21:15.423
Keep the engine fixed at Boltz-2.

103
00:21:15.583 --> 00:21:21.428
WT means wild type: the starting nanobody,
before we changed any amino acids.

104
00:21:21.588 --> 00:21:25.066
Its ipSAE is 0.055.

105
00:21:25.226 --> 00:21:27.466
We will read one mutant from the same run.

106
00:21:27.928 --> 00:21:35.758
Select RND_000001, the first mutant
in name order. Its ipSAE is 0.015.

107
00:21:35.918 --> 00:21:42.766
The interface is where the two proteins meet. Higher
ipSAE indicates more confidence in that interface.

108
00:21:42.926 --> 00:21:47.640
To measure how tightly these proteins bind,
you would need a laboratory experiment.

109
00:21:48.095 --> 00:21:52.319
Expand candidate details, then
Original model ID and sequence.

110
00:21:52.479 --> 00:21:55.786
Two changes distinguish this mutant from WT.

111
00:21:55.946 --> 00:21:58.847
At position 98, alanine became leucine.

112
00:21:59.007 --> 00:22:02.399
At position 110, glutamate became leucine.

113
00:22:02.559 --> 00:22:04.820
These positions belong to the nanobody.

114
00:22:04.980 --> 00:22:07.540
The hotspot numbers belong to EGFR.

115
00:22:08.012 --> 00:22:17.206
This model has an ipTM of 0.377
and an interface pLDDT of 79.335.

116
00:22:17.366 --> 00:22:22.422
ipTM tells us how sure the model is about
where one protein sits against the other.

117
00:22:22.582 --> 00:22:31.201
Interface pLDDT tells us how sure it is about the
local shapes at their meeting surface. Higher is more
confident for each score.

118
00:22:31.678 --> 00:22:33.534
Find Hotspot contact fraction.

119
00:22:33.694 --> 00:22:37.940
For this mutant, 3 of our three
target residues contact the nanobody.

120
00:22:38.100 --> 00:22:40.873
The recorded distance cutoff is 5 angstroms.

121
00:22:41.033 --> 00:22:44.532
These predicted contacts can
guide follow-up experiments.

122
00:22:45.012 --> 00:22:52.841
Return to the structure above and drag to rotate it. The
two chains are the EGFR fragment and the mutant nanobody.

123
00:22:53.001 --> 00:22:56.905
This shows the predicted orientation
for the selected candidate.

124
00:22:57.887 --> 00:23:05.140
Below the viewer, click CDR3 97–113. This
highlights a loop at the tip of the nanobody.

125
00:23:05.300 --> 00:23:07.412
We allowed changes only in this loop.

126
00:23:07.572 --> 00:23:10.644
See where it sits relative to the EGFR surface.

127
00:23:11.095 --> 00:23:13.228
Turn on the hotspot and contact layers.

128
00:23:13.388 --> 00:23:16.716
The hotspot marks the place you
asked the nanobody to reach.

129
00:23:16.876 --> 00:23:22.807
Contacts show where the predicted proteins actually
meet. Use Full structure to see them in context.

130
00:23:31.553 --> 00:23:36.631
The mean interface PAE for this
mutant is 12.419 angstroms.

131
00:23:36.791 --> 00:23:42.487
Each square estimates uncertainty in the
position of one amino acid relative to another.

132
00:23:42.647 --> 00:23:44.289
Lower means less uncertainty.

133
00:23:44.449 --> 00:23:48.481
To read the interface, compare a point
on one chain with a point on the other.

134
00:23:48.928 --> 00:23:52.960
Open the saved selection criteria
in this candidate’s details.

135
00:23:53.120 --> 00:23:57.494
Read the measured value, the threshold,
and the pass or fail column together.

136
00:23:57.654 --> 00:24:00.214
This mutant is recorded as ineligible.

137
00:24:02.220 --> 00:24:07.255
Open section four, Exclusions. One model
can be counted for several reasons.

138
00:24:07.415 --> 00:24:13.303
All ten mutants fell below both the
ipSAE and ipTM thresholds for this run.

139
00:24:13.463 --> 00:24:18.668
All ten were also flagged for developability
review. Three had a predicted clash.

140
00:24:18.828 --> 00:24:25.079
All eleven models were calculated. Zero selected
means no mutant passed the saved selection rules.

141
00:24:25.553 --> 00:24:28.988
Before sharing, return to section
two and find Handoff check.

142
00:24:29.148 --> 00:24:30.599
Handoff is incomplete.

143
00:24:30.759 --> 00:24:35.985
You can export the report for discussion and review
the listed blockers before approving release.

144
00:24:36.145 --> 00:24:38.641
Here it records 7 errors and 1 warning.

145
00:24:38.801 --> 00:24:40.529
This checks the files you can share.

146
00:24:40.689 --> 00:24:44.401
To learn whether the proteins really
bind, you need a lab experiment.

147
00:24:44.845 --> 00:24:46.872
Here the expression vector is missing.

148
00:24:47.032 --> 00:24:51.426
This is the DNA carrier the laboratory
would use to produce the nanobody.

149
00:24:51.586 --> 00:24:57.133
You can share the report for discussion, then
resolve the listed items before ordering synthesis.

150
00:24:57.595 --> 00:25:04.379
Open Results database. Enter the same name you
typed at the start: EGFR 10 variants walkthrough.

151
00:25:04.539 --> 00:25:09.638
Click Search. In the first column, click
the analysis name to open the saved result.

152
00:25:14.720 --> 00:25:19.776
The link opens the same saved result in
a new tab. Scroll to Save all results.

153
00:25:19.936 --> 00:25:25.205
Choose the delivery report ZIP, which includes
the structures and PAE, then click Save.

154
00:25:58.553 --> 00:26:02.628
On the computer where you downloaded
the ZIP, open a terminal in Downloads.

155
00:26:02.788 --> 00:26:08.313
The Python command on screen extracts the
entire archive into NanoCoFold-report.

156
00:26:08.473 --> 00:26:15.151
Inside are report.html, the report, and
candidates.csv, the table of candidates.

157
00:26:17.762 --> 00:26:21.346
Open report.html in the extracted folder.

158
00:26:21.506 --> 00:26:26.818
You can double-click the file, or use the terminal
command shown here to open it in your browser.

159
00:26:27.262 --> 00:26:31.422
This report is open from the extracted
file, with the browser offline.

160
00:26:31.582 --> 00:26:37.512
The same analysis name, model counts, rotatable
structure and PAE map are still available.

161
00:26:37.672 --> 00:26:40.979
Send the full ZIP so your
colleague can open the same report.

